In this lesson, we'll look at how to read and interpret graphs made from real-world data. A line graph puts one variable on the horizontal axis, that's the x-axis, and another on the vertical axis, the y-axis. The independent variable, the one we change or measure first, goes on the horizontal, and the dependent variable, the one that responds, goes on the vertical.

The data we use comes from authentic sources, like temperature readings, ticket sales, or water usage, not from a made-up textbook rule. To find a y-value for a given x, you go up from the x-axis to the line, then across to the y-axis. To find an x-value for a given y, you go across from the y-axis to the line, then down.

When describing the relationship, look for the trend, is the line going up, down, or staying flat? Look at the shape, whether it's straight or curved. Note the key points, like where it starts, crosses an axis, or reaches a peak. And check the rate, how quickly y changes as x changes.

Just remember, if two things move together, it doesn't mean one causes the other. Now let's see it in action.